= Solution
{parent=4}

<Itô formula> gives
$$
dM_t=M_t\nabla f(B_t)\cdot dB_t,
$$
so $M$ is a positive <local martingale>. On every finite interval $[0,T]$, the hypotheses imply
$$
\int_0^T|\nabla f(B_s)|^2ds
\leq C_T\left(1+\sup_{s\leq T}|B_s|^{2-\epsilon}\right).
$$
The <Gaussian tail bound> of the Brownian maximum has finite exponential moments of every subquadratic power. Therefore <Novikov condition> holds on $[0,T]$, and the <stochastic exponential> $M$ is a true martingale there. Since $T$ was arbitrary, **$M$ is a true martingale**.

